<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bukovina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bukovina is a historical region on the north-eastern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, divided today between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Part of the Principality of Moldavia, it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy in 1775 and became a separate crown land in 1849, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Czernowitz</a>. It joined Romania in 1918 and was split in 1940, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed the north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukovina.jpg" alt="Bukovina" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adilena · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voronet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bukovina lies on the northern slopes of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-carpathians">Eastern Carpathians</a> and the adjoining plains, at the crossroads of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. Bukovina proper covers 10,442 square kilometres. Its name first appears in a document of the Voivode of Moldavia Roman I Mușat of 30 March 1392 and comes from the Slavic word buk, beech tree; the German name Buchenland and the Romanian Țara Fagilor both mean land of beech trees. The region was long inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> in the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> in the north, and it was known for its ethnic variety.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of settlement go back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, and the Trypillian culture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> and Getae followed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> appearing by the 4th century and Slavic people in the 5th and 6th. From the late 10th century Bukovina became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and then of the Principality of Galicia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. In the 14th century it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and became part of the Principality of Moldavia; the voivode Dragoș is named as the founder of Moldavia, whose name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova-river">Moldova River</a> in Bukovina. Suceava, in the south, was its capital from the late 14th century to the mid-16th century.</p><p>In 1497 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen III of Moldavia</a> defeated an army of King John I Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at the Battle of the Cosmin Forest, and in 1514 Moldavia became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Under Stephen the Great and his successors the painted monasteries of Moldovița, Sucevița, Putna, Humor and Voroneț were built.</p><p>After the Russo-Ottoman Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca of 21 July 1774, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy occupied the region in October 1774, and the Convention of Pera of 7 May 1775 ceded it. It was a district of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a> until it became the Duchy of Bukovina in 1849. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> to record ethnicity, in 1851, counted 380,826 people, 48.5% of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and 38.1% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>. Russian armies occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Czernowitz</a> three times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> collapsed in 1918 the Romanian army took the province, against Ukrainian protest. In 1930 it had 839,500 inhabitants, 43% Romanian, 28% Ukrainian, 11% Jewish and 9% German.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> demanded Bukovina from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on 26 June 1940, and Romania ceded the northern part, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Cernăuți</a>, on 28 June. Romania held the north again from 1941 as an ally of Nazi Germany, but the Soviet army retook it in 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Northern Bukovina is now the Chernivtsi Oblast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and southern Bukovina is part of Suceava County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Several of the painted monasteries are World Heritage Sites, among the painted churches of northern Moldavia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bukovina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The plains of Hungary were among the great grain-producing regions of Europe, and Galicia, <b>Bukovina</b>, Dalmatia, Transylvania and, after 1878, Bosnia-Herzegovina had predominantly rural economies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The monarchy also took part in the First Partition of Poland in 1772, acquiring Galicia, and gained <b>Bukovina</b> in 1775.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the First World War assemblies in Transylvania, Bessarabia and <b>Bukovina</b> voted for union with Romania, and the Treaty of Saint-Germain, the Treaty of Trianon and the Treaty of Paris of 1920 assigned most of these lands to it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bessarabia, annexed by Russia in 1812, became in 1991 the core of the independent Republic of Moldova, northern <b>Bukovina</b> and southern Bessarabia belong to Ukraine, and western Moldavia forms the north-east of Romania, where Iași remains a leading university city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Named <b>Bukovina</b>, the beech land, it was ruled by the military until 1786, then attached to Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bukovina">Duchy of Bukovina</a></li></ul></section>
